WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.375
Hello, my name is Eric Chow,

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:11.744
and I direct the UCSF Center for Advanced Technology.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:15.281
In this video, I'll show you how to use magnetic purification beads

00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:18.284
-- commonly referred to as AMPure or SPRI beads --

00:00:18.285 --> 00:00:23.689
that are commonly used in next-generation sequencing library preparation.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.958
These beads are magnetic particles

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:28.794
that are carboxy coated,

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:32.298
and contain... and are in a solution that contains PEG and sodium chloride.

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.967
The PEG and sodium chloride cause nucleic acids

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.903
to precipitate out of solution

00:00:36.904 --> 00:00:39.472
and use the beads as a nucleation point.

00:00:39.473 --> 00:00:41.474
And once they're stuck on the beads,

00:00:41.475 --> 00:00:43.376
they can be manipulated with magnets.

00:00:43.377 --> 00:00:45.711
What's nice about these SPRI beads

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:47.980
is that they can be tuned

00:00:47.981 --> 00:00:50.917
so that you can get different sizes of nucleic acids

00:00:50.918 --> 00:00:54.887
binding to the beads by the ratio of this PEG solution that's added.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:58.191
So, a lot of these purifications

00:00:58.192 --> 00:01:00.159
are typically done in PCR tubes

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.627
and small magnets like this.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:03.462
But for the purpose of today's video,

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.165
we'll be using Eppendorf tubes, because they're a little bit easier to visualize,

00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:10.169
and this larger magnet.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.605
The first step is to take your sample

00:01:12.606 --> 00:01:19.078
and add some of the SPRI beads to the sample.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:22.682
When pipetting the SPRI beads, you want to pipette slowly,

00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:24.784
because the solution is very viscous

00:01:24.785 --> 00:01:34.360
due to the high PEG concentration.

00:01:34.361 --> 00:01:36.596
When you add the mixture to your sample,

00:01:36.597 --> 00:01:39.799
you can mix by pipetting up and down,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.100
or you can vortex.

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:44.470
You just want to make sure you get a nice homogeneous suspension.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:46.539
And if you vortex,

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.175
you'll want to give it a quick spin in a benchtop centrifuge,

00:01:49.176 --> 00:01:59.819
just to collect all the fluids to the bottom of the tube.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.923
We'll want to let the beads sit, and the solution to sit,

00:02:03.924 --> 00:02:05.858
for about five minutes at room temperature

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:10.463
to give the nucleic acids time to bind to the surface of the beads.

00:02:10.464 --> 00:02:14.300
So, now five minutes has elapsed,

00:02:14.301 --> 00:02:17.603
and we'll put the sample onto the magnetic rack.

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:20.106
Depending on the strength of your magnet

00:02:20.107 --> 00:02:21.941
and the volume of beads that you've used,

00:02:21.942 --> 00:02:25.211
this can take anywhere from two minutes to five minutes for the beads,

00:02:25.212 --> 00:02:27.246
which now contain the nucleic acids,

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:29.415
to move to the back of the tube,

00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:32.552
attracted to the magnet.

00:02:32.553 --> 00:02:35.555
You'll know this is complete when your solution is clear

00:02:35.556 --> 00:02:43.396
and all the beads have been pulled out of solution.

00:02:43.397 --> 00:02:45.765
Now, several minutes have elapsed,

00:02:45.766 --> 00:02:47.533
and, if you can see,

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:49.902
the beads are now stuck to the back of the tube,

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:52.738
and the nucleic acids that are stuck onto the beads

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:54.440
have also been pulled out of solution.

00:02:54.441 --> 00:02:56.642
The next step is to remove the supernatant,

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.311
which now contains...

00:02:58.312 --> 00:03:07.653
does not contain any of your sample.

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:09.422
When you remove the supernatant,

00:03:09.423 --> 00:03:17.797
be careful not to disturb the beads at the back of the tube.

00:03:17.798 --> 00:03:24.670
You can discard the supernatant and perform the first wash with 80% ethanol.

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:26.906
Some protocols may call for a different percentage of ethanol,

00:03:26.907 --> 00:03:31.143
so just consult your protocol.

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:33.012
When you add the ethanol wash,

00:03:33.013 --> 00:03:34.814
you want to be careful not to disturb the pellet.

00:03:34.815 --> 00:03:36.949
So, you're not resuspending the beads

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.584
or mixing them into solution.

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:41.387
You're simply just adding ethanol to the tube

00:03:41.388 --> 00:03:44.390
while the beads are still stuck to the back of the tube.

00:03:44.391 --> 00:03:48.094
This is used to wash out any of the residual salt and PEG

00:03:48.095 --> 00:03:49.462
present around the beads.

00:03:49.463 --> 00:03:53.165
You want to let this sit for 30 seconds.

00:03:53.166 --> 00:04:09.248
After 30 seconds have elapsed, remove the first wash.

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:11.350
And then add the second ethanol wash to the tubes,

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:23.129
again making sure you don't disturb the pellets.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.098
30 seconds if elapsed, and we'll remove the second wash.

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:31.070
Try to remove all the residual ethanol from the bottom of the tube

00:04:31.071 --> 00:04:39.779
to aid drying.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.182
Now, you'll want to let the beads dry for about five minutes at room temperature.

00:04:43.183 --> 00:04:46.452
This allows the excess ethanol that's still present on the beads

00:04:46.453 --> 00:04:48.187
to evaporate away.

00:04:48.188 --> 00:04:50.189
And depending on the volume of beads that you used

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.457
and the size of your tube,

00:04:51.458 --> 00:04:53.960
sometimes this drying might happen faster.

00:04:53.961 --> 00:04:56.329
During this time, if you notice that the beads

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:00.299
are starting to get dry and not glistening any more,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.201
that's an indication that the beads are fully dry,

00:05:02.202 --> 00:05:03.903
and it's ready to go into elution.

00:05:03.904 --> 00:05:06.806
So, if you see them starting to turn this...

00:05:06.807 --> 00:05:08.641
kind of a dry, cakey brown color,

00:05:08.642 --> 00:05:14.580
you'll want to pull 'em off the magnet and go directly to elution.

00:05:14.581 --> 00:05:16.215
Five minutes have elapsed,

00:05:16.216 --> 00:05:18.284
and now we'll resuspend the beads

00:05:18.285 --> 00:05:20.419
with our elution buffer.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.789
Elution buffers simply are just water

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.159
or a low concentration of Tris or Tris-EDTA,

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.564
so they don't contain high concentrations of salts or PEG.

00:05:31.565 --> 00:05:34.033
And this causes DNA that's present on the beads

00:05:34.034 --> 00:05:35.868
to resuspend back into solution.

00:05:35.869 --> 00:05:40.573
And so, what we'll do is make sure we resuspend the beads

00:05:40.574 --> 00:05:42.475
and get rid of any chunks,

00:05:42.476 --> 00:05:45.011
and break them up.

00:05:45.012 --> 00:05:48.247
And again, you do this by pipetting up and down, or you can vortex.

00:05:48.248 --> 00:05:50.783
If you vortex, just make sure to do a quick spin afterwards

00:05:50.784 --> 00:05:59.091
to collect the liquid at the bottom of the tube.

00:05:59.092 --> 00:06:02.028
And we'll let this sit for 30 seconds to give the nucleic acids

00:06:02.029 --> 00:06:08.701
a little bit more time to come off the beads and into solution.

00:06:08.702 --> 00:06:10.436
After 30 seconds have elapsed,

00:06:10.437 --> 00:06:13.806
place the tube back on the rack

00:06:13.807 --> 00:06:17.510
to pull the beads that now do not contain nucleic acids out of solution,

00:06:17.511 --> 00:06:20.379
leaving the nucleic acids in your sample in the solution.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:24.717
So, this second bead-binding step to the magnet

00:06:24.718 --> 00:06:26.252
actually occurs a lot faster,

00:06:26.253 --> 00:06:28.020
because our elution buffer doesn't contain PEG,

00:06:28.021 --> 00:06:29.622
which is very viscous.

00:06:29.623 --> 00:06:31.757
So, in the initial separation,

00:06:31.758 --> 00:06:34.627
it took several minutes because the high concentration to PEG

00:06:34.628 --> 00:06:37.029
slowed the movement of the beads towards the magnets.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:39.999
And you can see, already, our beads are stuck to the back of the tube,

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:48.941
and we're ready to pull off our eluates.

00:06:48.942 --> 00:06:51.177
You want to carefully remove your sample,

00:06:51.178 --> 00:06:54.146
and again, not disturb the bead pellet at the back.

00:06:54.147 --> 00:06:56.282
If you notice that you've pulled up some beads,

00:06:56.283 --> 00:06:57.884
it's okay.

00:06:57.885 --> 00:06:59.852
Just go ahead and put your sample back in the tube

00:06:59.853 --> 00:07:02.522
and let the sample sit for a few seconds

00:07:02.523 --> 00:07:09.228
to allow the beads to stick back to the magnets.

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:11.430
So, now our eluate, with our purified sample,

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:13.799
is now in our new tube,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.469
and we've completed our cleanup.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:33.654
Thanks for watching.

